From c39fce0874f726325a19974a38188b3852d238bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 20:24:46 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 16/18] index: reconcile the loaded chain with the node before
 serving

The Electrum package lets an operator switch between nodes on the two sides of a chain
fork, which share every block below the split. It used to wipe the index on a switch,
which cost one user a 36-hour rebuild for a chain that shared all but ~6000 blocks with
the old one. The package now keeps the index; this is what makes that safe.

At startup, before the server loop handles anything, the index tip is checked against
the node: one `getblockhash` in the usual case, and if the node's active chain does not
contain it, a binary search (about twenty calls on mainnet) for the highest height both
agree on, and the header chain is trimmed back to there. Sound because agreement is
monotone -- a block hash commits to its ancestors, so chains that diverge never agree
again, and a shorter node agrees with nothing above its tip.

For two independently synced nodes `getheaders` already finds the common ancestor,
because neither knows the other's post-split blocks. What this adds there is
precision: the locator thins out exponentially, so the node's answer overshoots the
split by up to its depth again; this lands on it exactly.

The case it is needed for is a node that knows the indexed tip, has it off its active
chain, and descending from its own tip -- what `invalidateblock` leaves, the usual way
an operator picks a side of a fork. Core answers that locator with nothing. electrs
then keeps the rejected chain, advertises its tip, and the first query needing one of
those blocks asks the node for it: the node stays silent and electrs hangs, or drops the
connection and electrs exits, onto the same chain on restart.

I first described that hazard more broadly, as any switch to a node behind the fork,
and the regtest run corrected me: a node that never saw the other chain's blocks handles
the locator fine. The comment says only what the run showed.

Rows written for discarded blocks stay, as they do for every electrs reorg: rows are
keyed by height and a lookup scans this chain's block at that height, so a leftover row
costs a wasted scan, never a wrong answer. Nothing is persisted; until indexing writes a
new tip a restart reloads the old chain and trims it again.

Tests: the search is a pure function with six tests, including every boundary for
every tip up to 69, a failed RPC surfacing as an error rather than a disagreement, and
the tip fast path costing one call; three mutations each caught. End to end,
spikes/harness/chain_switch.py drives two diverging regtest nodes through a switch, a
switch back, an invalidateblock below the fork and a reconsiderblock, 13/13; and against
a build without this call it shows the rejected tip advertised and the next query hung.
