     // Return `Ok(true)` when the chain is fully synced and the index is compacted.
+    /// Trim the loaded header chain back to the last block the node also has on its active chain.
+    ///
+    /// The index on disk was built against whichever node electrs was pointed at when it was built,
+    /// and that need not be the node it is pointed at now. The Electrum package lets an operator
+    /// switch between nodes on the two sides of a chain fork, which share every block below the
+    /// split and none above it. Keeping the shared part is worth a great deal -- it is nearly the
+    /// whole index -- but only if nothing from the other side is ever served as this one's.
+    ///
+    /// Usually `getheaders` sorts that out by itself. Two nodes synced independently do not know
+    /// each other's blocks past the split, so the node skips electrs's recent locator entries as
+    /// unknown, finds a shared one, and sends everything after it. What this adds there is
+    /// precision: the locator's entries thin out exponentially, so the node's answer overshoots
+    /// the split by up to its depth again, and this lands on it exactly.
+    ///
+    /// The case `getheaders` cannot handle is a node that *knows* the indexed tip, has it off its
+    /// active chain, and has it descending from its own tip. That is what `invalidateblock`
+    /// leaves behind, which is the usual way an operator picks a side of a fork. Bitcoin Core
+    /// answers such a locator with nothing, so electrs keeps the rejected chain and advertises its
+    /// tip to wallets. The first query that needs one of those blocks asks the node for it; the
+    /// node will not send a block it rejected, and electrs either hangs on the silence or exits
+    /// when the connection drops -- then reloads the same chain on restart. Reproduced against
+    /// real nodes, with and without this, in `spikes/harness/chain_switch.py`.
+    ///
+    /// So the chain is checked against the node before anything is served: the tip first, which
+    /// is one RPC and the only one in the usual case, then a binary search for the highest height
+    /// where both agree. That is sound because agreement is monotone -- a block hash commits to
+    /// all of its ancestors, so two chains that diverge never agree again, and a shorter node
+    /// agrees with nothing above its tip.
+    ///
+    /// Rows written for the discarded blocks stay in the database. That is how electrs treats
+    /// every reorg: rows are keyed by height, a lookup resolves the height to this chain's block
+    /// and scans that block, so a leftover row costs a wasted scan and never a wrong answer.
+    ///
+    /// Nothing is persisted. Until indexing overwrites the stored tip, a restart loads the old
+    /// chain and trims it again, which is idempotent.
+    pub(crate) fn reconcile_with_node(&mut self, daemon: &Daemon) -> Result<()> {
+        let tip = self.chain.height();
+        if tip == 0 {
+            return Ok(()); // nothing indexed yet
+        }
+        let chain = &self.chain;
+        let agreed = last_agreed_height(tip, |height| {
+            Ok(daemon.get_block_hash_at(height)? == chain.get_block_hash(height))
+        })
+        .context("failed to compare the index with the node's chain")?;
+        if agreed < tip {
+            warn!(
+                "the node does not have the indexed tip at height {} on its active chain; the two \
+                 last agree at height {}, so indexing resumes from there",
+                tip, agreed
+            );
+            self.chain.drop_last_headers(tip - agreed);
+            self.stats.observe_chain(&self.chain);
+        }
+        Ok(())
+    }
+
     pub(crate) fn sync(&mut self, daemon: &Daemon, exit_flag: &ExitFlag) -> Result<bool> {
